notes               = "Untitled in source. The dedication is printed in the outer margin in two lines: با یاد محمدرضا فرهادی ('in memory of Mohammad-Reza Farhadi') and, in guillemets, «هنرجوی عاشقی که دیدم و ندیدم» ('a lovelorn art student whom I saw and did not see') — the poet's own gloss on a student of his. Line 18 is a joke in grammatical terms: بی‌مبتدا / بی‌قید / بی‌خبر are subject, adverb and predicate — a sentence with no grammar left — while بی‌خبر also just means 'unaware'; only one of the two survives translation. The stone and the inscription are a grave. 17 Aban = 7 November 1996. Imported as a draft with machine / lantern layers; ===translation=== left for Ben."
draft               = true

===persian===

لب باز کن...
با نگاه به این سنگ،
بَراین کتیبهٔ همیشه دلتنگ...
لب باز کن...
با نگاه به این گل...
یا با نگاه به سبزه...
اینک کنارِ تو...
ترکیبِ سبز و خاک و... خطّ و خاطره...
کاری بکن که هیچ نکردی...
راهی برو که هیچ نرفتی...
شعری بخوان... نگفته... نخوانده...
لب باز کن...
چیزی بگو که هیچ نگفتی...
این‌بار در هُرمِ هاله... هالهٔ دیدار...
روح مرا رها کن... از خمودیِ تکرار
یک جمله... یک پدیدهٔ تازه...
بی‌مبتدا... بی‌قید... بی‌خبر...
با قلب سرخ... اما... و با نگاهِ تر
ای آهِ من... دعای مرا مستجاب کن...
هرکس که آفرید... گناهش ثواب کن
۱۳۷۵/۸/۱۷

===machine===

Open your lips...
with a look at this stone,
at this inscription that is always grieving...
Open your lips...
with a look at this flower...
or with a look at the grass...
Here now, beside you...
a compound of green and earth and... line and memory...
Do a thing you have never done...
Go a road you have never gone...
Sing a poem... unsaid... unsung...
Open your lips...
Say a thing you have never said...
This time in the shimmer of a halo... the halo of meeting...
Let my soul go... out of the torpor of repetition...
One sentence... one new-made thing...
no subject... no adverb... no predicate...
with a red heart... and yet... and with a wet gaze...
O my own sigh... grant my prayer...
and whoever made it... count his sin a merit.
1375/8/17

===lantern===

Open your lips —
with a look at this stone,
at this inscription that is always grieving.
Open your lips —
with a look at this flower,
or with a look at the grass.
Here now, beside you:
a compound of green and earth, of line and memory.
Do a thing you have never done.
Go a road you have never gone.
Sing a poem unsaid, unsung.
Open your lips.
Say a thing you have never said.
This time, in the shimmer of a halo, the halo of meeting,
let my soul go out of the torpor of repetition —
one sentence, one new-made thing,
with no subject, no adverb, no predicate,
with a red heart, and yet, and with a wet looking.
O my own sigh — grant my prayer,
and whoever made it, count his sin a merit.

1375/8/17

===translation===

===footnotes===

بی‌مبتدا، بی‌قید، بی‌خبر names the three parts of a Persian sentence — subject, adverb,
predicate — and asks for an utterance with none of them. The last of the three, بی‌خبر, is also the
everyday word for *unaware*, so the line simultaneously asks for a sentence with no grammar and for
a state with no knowledge. The English keeps the grammar and loses the innocence.
